    "result_text": "Build is green. Here's what I implemented and why.\n\n## Change\n\n`membershipNumber` is `'<customerCode>-M<YY>'`, where `YY` is the last two digits of the `registrationDate` year \u2014 a value derived entirely from the owner's own persisted fields. That makes it exactly like the existing `displayName` and `initials` fields: a read-only projection computed at serialization time, not persisted state that needs a pipeline step and DB column (as `customerCode`/`namesakeCount` do, since those depend on cross-owner state at creation).\n\nSo I put it where derived response fields already live:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `membershipNumber` property to the `Owner` schema (next to `namesakeCount`), which generates the field on `OwnerDto`.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping(... expression = \"java(membershipNumber(owner))\")` and a small `membershipNumber(Owner)` default method alongside `displayName`/`initials`:\n   ```java\n   default String membershipNumber(Owner owner) {\n       String customerCode = owner.getCustomerCode();\n       LocalDate registrationDate = owner.getRegistrationDate();\n       if (customerCode == null || registrationDate == null) {\n           return null;\n       }\n       return String.format(\"%s-M%02d\", customerCode, registrationDate.getYear() % 100);\n   }\n   ```\n   `getYear() % 100` with `%02d` gives the zero-padded last two digits (e.g. 2026 \u2192 `26`, 2007 \u2192 `07`). The null guard keeps GET responses safe for any owner missing either field, rather than throwing.\n\nNo new pipeline step or storage was needed \u2014 the value flows automatically into both the create response and every `GET`, keeping each unit single-purpose and reusing the mapper's existing derived-field pattern.\n\nFull test suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, all tests green including the new `coreMembershipNumberFormat`.",
